f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339813" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{13}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{13}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339813" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339813" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339813" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{19}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{19}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{19}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339813" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{19}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-4y,\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56bac6f3defd32391a6887b7137fe74160344893f525a71cb361b"} {"question" "q-339820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}-4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{17}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}-4{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-4\\cdot \\frac{17}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{17}{32}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}-4{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-4\\cdot \\frac{17}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{64}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{64}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{35}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{35}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042033/state.json" "file_sha256" "e91f0265fbb56b